insert into public.devotionals (n, title, body, today, pray) values
(26, $t$No other name$t$, $t$Peter said this to the same leaders who had crucified Jesus. It was a bold claim then, and it still is today.

In a world that says all paths lead to God, this verse can feel uncomfortable. But it's also why evangelism matters. If there were many ways to be saved, we could leave people where they are. Because Jesus is the only way, telling people about Him is an act of love.

We can hold this truth with humility. We aren't saved because we're better. We're saved because of His name, not ours.$t$, $t$Pray for someone you know who is searching in other places. Ask God to show them Jesus.$t$, $t$Jesus, thank You that Your name saves. Help me share it with love.$t$),
(27, $t$Patient with us$t$, $t$God's timing can feel slow. We pray for years. We wonder why He hasn't acted yet.

Peter says what looks like slowness is actually patience. God is giving people time because He doesn't want any of them to perish. That patience once reached you. It's reaching the people you're praying for right now.

If God is that patient, we can be patient too. We don't need to rush people, pressure them, or give up on them. We can keep loving, praying, and showing up, trusting that God's timing is better than ours.

Every day the Lord waits is another day of mercy for someone you love.$t$, $t$Is there someone you've been tempted to give up on? Set one gentle next step for them this week.$t$, $t$Lord, make me patient with people the way You've been patient with me.$t$),
(28, $t$While we were yet sinners$t$, $t$God didn't wait for us to clean up before He loved us. Christ died for us while we were still sinners.

That's the heart of the gospel, and it shapes how we treat people. We don't wait for others to get their lives together before we love them. We love them right where they are, because that's how God loved us.

Some of the people you're tending are in messy places. They may have habits or opinions that make you uncomfortable. God's love reached you in your mess. It can reach them in theirs.$t$, $t$Think of someone you've held at a distance until they "get it together." Reach out this week anyway.$t$, $t$Father, thank You for loving me before I changed. Help me love others the same way.$t$),
(29, $t$The gift of God$t$, $t$This verse sets two things side by side: wages and a gift. Wages are earned. A gift is received.

Sin pays exactly what it promises, and the wage is death. But God doesn't offer us a better wage. He offers a gift: eternal life, freely given through Jesus.

Many people you know think of God as a boss keeping score. They're trying to earn His approval or they've given up trying. The good news is that eternal life can't be earned. It can only be received.

That's a simple, clear way to explain the gospel to someone. Wages versus gift.$t$, $t$Practice explaining this verse in your own words, in under a minute.$t$, $t$Thank You, Father, for the gift I could never earn.$t$),
(30, $t$Confess and believe$t$, $t$Salvation isn't complicated. Believe in your heart that God raised Jesus from the dead. Confess with your mouth that Jesus is Lord.

Sometimes we make it sound harder than it is, as if people have to understand everything first. They don't. They need to trust Jesus and turn to Him as Lord.

Be ready for the moment someone says, "So what do I do?" Many believers freeze right there. This verse gives a clear answer. You can even pray with them right then.

It's a moment worth being ready for.$t$, $t$Think through how you'd help someone who says, "I want to follow Jesus." Write down what you'd say.$t$, $t$Lord, prepare me for the moment someone is ready.$t$),
(31, $t$A new creature$t$, $t$The gospel doesn't just improve people. It makes them new.

This is hope for the people you're tending who feel stuck: stuck in addiction, in shame, in patterns they've tried to break for years. In Christ, the old can pass away.

It's also a reminder about how we see new believers. Don't keep them tied to who they used to be. God doesn't. Treat them as the new people He has made them.

No one is too far gone to be made new. You're living proof.$t$, $t$Pray for someone who feels stuck, asking God to make them new.$t$, $t$Lord, thank You for making me new. Do it for them too.$t$),
(32, $t$By grace$t$, $t$Grace means getting what we don't deserve. Salvation isn't a reward for good people. It's a gift for sinners who trust Jesus.

That truth humbles us. We have nothing to boast about. We aren't better than the people we're reaching. We're beggars telling other beggars where to find bread.

It also gives us hope for everyone. If salvation depended on works, some people would be out of reach. Because it's by grace, anyone can receive it.

Let grace shape how you talk to people. Speak as someone who has received it, not as someone who earned it.$t$, $t$Before your next spiritual conversation, remember what grace has done for you. Let it soften your tone.$t$, $t$Lord, keep me humble and amazed by Your grace.$t$),
(33, $t$Through Christ$t$, $t$Paul wrote this from prison while talking about contentment. He had learned to live with plenty and with little, because Christ gave him strength.

This verse isn't a promise that we can do anything we want. It's a promise that we can do anything God calls us to, because He strengthens us.

That includes the hard parts of loving people: being patient with someone difficult, having the conversation you've been avoiding, or continuing to pray when you're tired.

You won't always feel strong. You don't have to. His strength is enough.$t$, $t$What's one hard thing God is asking of you with someone? Ask Him for strength, then do it.$t$, $t$Jesus, give me Your strength for what You've called me to.$t$),
(34, $t$The way, the truth, the life$t$, $t$Thomas asked Jesus, "How can we know the way?" Jesus didn't hand him a map. He said, "I am the way."

Christianity isn't first a set of rules or ideas. It's a person. People don't just need better information about God. They need to meet Jesus.

That's why it helps to point people to Jesus Himself, not just church or morality. Read the Gospels together. Let them see how He talked, loved, and acted. People who meet Jesus in the Gospels often find Him more compelling than they expected.$t$, $t$Invite someone to read one of the Gospels with you, maybe a chapter a week over coffee.$t$, $t$Jesus, help me point people to You, not just to ideas about You.$t$),
(35, $t$Life more abundantly$t$, $t$Jesus contrasts Himself with a thief. The thief takes. Jesus gives.

Many people believe the opposite. They think following Jesus means losing their freedom and fun. But Jesus says He came to give a full, abundant life, and that sin is what really steals.

The people you're tending have felt the thief's work: broken relationships, regret, emptiness. Jesus offers something better than anything the world offers.

Your life can show this. When people see joy, peace, and purpose in you, it makes His promise believable.$t$, $t$Ask someone you're tending what would make life feel full to them. Listen closely.$t$, $t$Jesus, let my life show the abundant life You give.$t$),
(36, $t$Come unto me$t$, $t$Jesus' invitation is for the tired. Not the impressive, the put-together, or the religious. The weary.

Many people around you are exhausted. Worn out by work, family stress, grief, or trying to hold everything together. They may not be asking about God, but they are longing for rest.

This verse is one of the gentlest ways to introduce Jesus. You can simply say, "There's a verse that's helped me when I'm worn out," and share it.

And don't forget: this invitation is for you too. You can't give rest you haven't received.$t$, $t$Is someone you're tending worn out? Send them this verse with a note that you're praying for them.$t$, $t$Jesus, I come to You for rest. Help others find it too.$t$),
(37, $t$I stand at the door$t$, $t$Jesus pictures Himself standing at a door and knocking. He doesn't break in. He waits to be invited, and what He wants is a meal together, real friendship.

God is patient and personal. He pursues people, but He doesn't force them.

We can follow His example. We knock through kindness, invitations, and honest conversations. We don't push our way in. And when someone opens the door to us, it's often over a table.

Keep knocking gently. And keep pointing to the One who knocks.$t$, $t$Knock on a door this week, literally or figuratively. Invite someone for a meal.$t$, $t$Lord, help me knock patiently, and open hearts to You.$t$),
(38, $t$All things to all men$t$, $t$Paul adapted to the people he was reaching. He didn't change the gospel. He changed his approach.

That means meeting people where they are. Talking to a sports fan about the game. Showing up to a neighbor's cookout. Learning what matters to a coworker before trying to talk about faith.

It also means being humble enough to sit with people in their weakness. Paul says, "To the weak became I as weak." We don't come as experts. We come alongside.

"By all means" shows how much Paul cared. He would try anything faithful to reach people.$t$, $t$Look at the interests you've logged for someone. Plan a next step built around something they love.$t$, $t$Lord, make me flexible in my approach and faithful in my message.$t$),
(39, $t$The God of hope$t$, $t$Paul calls God "the God of hope." Hope is part of who He is.

Our witness flows from hope. When we're discouraged about someone, it shows. When we're filled with hope, people notice that too.

Paul prays that we would "abound" in hope, overflowing with it. That kind of hope doesn't come from looking at circumstances. It comes from believing God and from the power of the Holy Spirit.

If you're low on hope today, don't fake it. Ask the God of hope to fill you.$t$, $t$Read this verse as a prayer over yourself, then pray it over one person you're tending.$t$, $t$God of hope, fill me with joy and peace today.$t$),
(40, $t$They that wait upon the LORD$t$, $t$Waiting is hard, especially when we're waiting on God to work in someone we love.

But Isaiah says waiting on the Lord isn't wasted time. It renews our strength. Waiting is trusting. It's choosing to keep looking to God instead of giving up.

Notice the progression: soar, run, walk. Some days you'll feel like you're flying. Other days you'll just keep walking. Both are faithfulness.

If you're in a long season of waiting for someone, God is strengthening you in it.$t$, $t$Bring God the name you've been waiting on the longest. Tell Him you'll keep waiting.$t$, $t$Lord, renew my strength while I wait on You.$t$),
(41, $t$New every morning$t$, $t$Jeremiah wrote this in the middle of a book full of grief. Jerusalem had fallen. Everything was lost. Yet he remembered God's mercies.

Every morning, God's mercy is fresh. Yesterday's failures don't use it up. That's true for you, and it's true for the people you're praying for.

Maybe yesterday's conversation went badly. Maybe you missed a chance to speak up. Today is new. God's mercy is new. Begin again.

The same is true for the people you're tending. Don't hold yesterday against them either.$t$, $t$Start fresh with someone where things have been awkward or strained. Reach out with kindness.$t$, $t$Lord, thank You for new mercy this morning.$t$),
(42, $t$This is the day$t$, $t$Today is not an accident. God made it, and He has put people in it for you to love.

It's easy to put off evangelism until a better day. When things calm down. When I know more. When the timing is right. But today is the day the Lord made.

Rejoicing in the day changes how we see people in it. Instead of interruptions, they become opportunities. The person who stops to talk, the coworker who needs a hand, the neighbor out walking: God made today with them in it.

Don't wait for a better day. This one is His.$t$, $t$Look at today's next steps. Do one of them before noon.$t$, $t$Lord, thank You for this day. Help me use it for You.$t$),
(43, $t$Pray without ceasing$t$, $t$Three words, and one of the hardest commands in Scripture. How can anyone pray all the time?

Paul doesn't mean we should stay on our knees all day. He means living in ongoing conversation with God. Short prayers while driving. A whispered "help me" before a hard conversation. A quick "bless her" when a friend's name pops up on your phone.

This kind of praying fits naturally into tending people. As you go through your day and see the people God has put in your life, pray for them right then. Short and often.

Prayer doesn't have to be long to be real.$t$, $t$Every time you see someone you're tending today, in person or on your phone, pray a one-sentence prayer for them.$t$, $t$Lord, keep me talking with You all day long.$t$),
(44, $t$Save a soul from death$t$, $t$James ends his letter talking about someone who has wandered from the truth. The person who brings them back saves a soul from death.

This verse is often about people who once followed Jesus and drifted away. You probably know some. A friend who stopped coming to church. A family member who used to believe.

It can feel awkward to reach out. We don't want to seem judgmental. But James says gently bringing someone back is one of the most loving things you can do.

Start with love and a relationship, not a lecture.$t$, $t$Think of someone who has drifted from faith. Reach out with a simple, warm message, with no agenda.$t$, $t$Lord, bring back the ones who have wandered.$t$),
(45, $t$He first loved us$t$, $t$Our love for God always starts with His love for us. We don't climb up to Him. He comes down to us.

The same is true in evangelism. We don't love people well because we're naturally loving. We love because we've been loved first.

When loving someone feels hard, go back to the source. Remember how God loved you before you loved Him, when you were difficult, distant, or uninterested.

Love that's been received is love that can be given away.$t$, $t$Spend two minutes thanking God for specific ways He has loved you. Then reach out to someone.$t$, $t$Father, fill me with Your love so I can give it away.$t$),
(46, $t$The same yesterday, today, and forever$t$, $t$The Jesus who touched lepers, ate with sinners, and called fishermen is the same Jesus today. He hasn't changed.

That means the Jesus we read about in the Gospels is the one we're introducing people to. He still welcomes the outsider. He still forgives the guilty. He still calls people to follow Him.

It also means His promises still hold. He was faithful to you in the past, He is faithful now, and He will be faithful tomorrow.

Whatever changes in your life or in the people you're tending, Jesus doesn't.$t$, $t$Read one story from the Gospels and ask, "How might Jesus do this same thing for someone I know?"$t$, $t$Jesus, thank You that You never change.$t$),
(47, $t$Beautiful feet$t$, $t$In ancient times, people waited anxiously for messengers to come over the mountains with news from a battle. When the news was good, even the runner's dusty feet looked beautiful.

That's how God sees those who carry the gospel. Your feet, walking to a neighbor's door or driving to meet a friend, are beautiful to Him.

The news you carry is the best news there is: peace with God, salvation, and the truth that God reigns.

No one is too ordinary to be a messenger.$t$, $t$Go somewhere this week specifically to be with someone you're tending.$t$, $t$Lord, make my feet beautiful as I carry Your good news.$t$),
(48, $t$I have chosen you$t$, $t$Jesus reminds His disciples that He chose them, and He chose them for a purpose: to go and bear fruit that lasts.

You aren't an accident in God's plan. He chose you, and He placed you among specific people for a reason.

Notice the kind of fruit He wants: fruit that remains. Not quick decisions that fade, but lives truly changed over time. That's why tending matters. Lasting fruit grows through patient relationships.

And He connects it to prayer. Ask the Father, in Jesus' name, for the fruit He wants to grow.$t$, $t$Ask God for lasting fruit in one relationship. Then take one patient step in it.$t$, $t$Jesus, thank You for choosing me. Let my fruit remain.$t$),
(49, $t$All men to be saved$t$, $t$Paul says it plainly: God wants all people to be saved. Not just the easy ones, the friendly ones, or the ones who already seem religious. All.

This shapes how we pray. Right before this verse, Paul urges prayer for everyone, even kings and those in authority. If God desires everyone's salvation, no one is outside our prayers.

It also shapes our hope. When you pray for someone's salvation, you're praying in line with God's own desire. You can pray boldly.

Who have you quietly left off your list? God hasn't left them off His.$t$, $t$Pray for someone you'd normally never think to pray for: a public leader, a difficult boss, someone you disagree with.$t$, $t$Lord, give me Your desire for everyone to be saved.$t$),
(50, $t$Pray the Lord of the harvest$t$, $t$Jesus saw crowds of people who were harassed and helpless. He told His disciples the harvest was huge, but there weren't enough workers.

His answer wasn't a recruitment campaign. It was prayer. Ask the Lord of the harvest to send workers.

Here's what often happens when people pray that prayer: God sends them. In the very next chapter, Jesus sends out the same disciples who had just prayed it.

Pray for more workers, and be ready to be one of the answers.$t$, $t$Pray for workers in your workplace, neighborhood, and family. Then ask God where He wants you working.$t$, $t$Lord of the harvest, send workers, and send me.$t$),
(51, $t$Except they be sent$t$, $t$Paul finishes his chain of questions. People can't call on Jesus without believing, can't believe without hearing, can't hear without someone telling, and no one tells unless they're sent.

Every believer has been sent. Some are sent across oceans. Most are sent across the street, the office, or the dinner table.

Being sent means your daily life has purpose. Your job, your neighborhood, and your friendships are where God has sent you.

You're not just living there. You're on assignment.$t$, $t$As you go to work or run errands today, remind yourself, "I'm sent here."$t$, $t$Lord, thank You for sending me. Help me live like it.$t$);